(* ============================================================================
   PART 6: INCLUDES
   ============================================================================ *)

(* PROCESSOR-LEVEL (CARVE-P6-001 below): includes are NOT part of the core parser
   and the directive is deliberately NOT reachable from `block`/`inline` --
   a conformant core may leave `{{ … }}` literal. *)
include_directive = "{{", space+, include_path, [[space+], include_section], [include_options], space+, "}}" ;
(* The padding is a RUN of spaces, not one space: at least one is required on
   EACH side, so `{{c.crv}}` and `{{ c.crv}}` are literal text. A tab is
   content (CARVE-P7-001), so `{{<TAB>c.crv<TAB>}}` is literal text too. A
   bare path stops at the first space; a quoted path carries its own
   delimiters. *)
(* THE CLOSER is the first "}}" that falls OUTSIDE any quoted run. A quoted run
   -- `quoted_include_path` here, or the `quoted_value` form of an option's
   `include_option_value` -- MAY contain the "}}" pair: both productions
   exclude only their own quote character, the backslash and the newline, so a
   "}}" between the quotes belongs to the quoted run and does NOT end the
   directive. Both spellings therefore close at the "}}" that FOLLOWS the
   closing quote, and what comes after it is ordinary text:

     {{ "a }} more" @k:v }} end
     {{ ch.crv @label:"a }} more" }} end

   An UNTERMINATED quote does not open a run. It falls back to the unquoted
   reading, so it can never pair with a quote further along the line, and the
   closer is again the first "}}" - which is what leaves a malformed directive
   as the literal text CARVE-P6-001 says it is. *)
include_path = bare_include_path | quoted_include_path ;
bare_include_path = character - ('"' | '#' | '@' | '}' | ' ' | newline), {character - ('#' | '@' | '}' | ' ' | newline)} ;
(* A path carrying a space is DOUBLE-QUOTED, and only then: the bare form stops
   at the first space, so an unquoted `my chapter.crv` ends the path at `my`
   and the rest is read as the option slot it is not. Inside the quoted form a
   backslash takes the next character with it and decodes per CARVE-P7-004:
   `"a\.crv"` names `a.crv` and `"notes\new.crv"` names `notes\new.crv`. *)
quoted_include_path = '"', {('\', character - newline) | (character - ('"' | '\' | newline))}+, '"' ;
include_section = id_attribute ;
(* The same spelling as `id_attribute`, so `explicit_identifier`, not
   `identifier`: the name selects by explicit id (I1a below), so every id
   `{#...}` can spell is nameable, digit-leading
   ones such as `{#2024-plan}` included. No new ambiguity: the bare path already
   stops at `#`, and neither identifier class holds a space, `@` or `}`. *)
include_options = {[space+], '@', identifier, [':', include_option_value]}+ ;
(* The value is optional in the shape so a bare `@code` (I1c) is well formed.
   Every other option takes a value: a bare `@shift` or `@region` is a
   malformed option (Warning + literal, I7). Every other `@key` is reserved: a
   processor that does not recognize one SHOULD treat the directive the same
   way. *)
include_option_value = include_unquoted_value | quoted_value ;
include_unquoted_value = ( character - '}' - '|' - '"' - "'" - '\' - '@' - ' ' - '\t' - newline )+ ;
(* `unquoted_value` (PART 4) less the `@`. An unquoted value ends at the next
   option's marker, so `@shift:1@lines:1-8` is two options and never one shift
   whose value is `1@lines:1-8`. A value that needs an `@` takes the quoted
   form. *)
(* The spaces before an `@` or a `#section` are OPTIONAL, in EVERY position. `bare_include_path`
   stops at `@` and `include_section` holds no `@` either, so both slots are
   already spelled to let an option butt straight up against them:
   `{{ c.crv@shift:1 }}` and `{{ c.crv #Alpha@shift:1 }}` are well formed, and so
   is the spaced `{{ c.crv @shift:1 @lines:1-2 }}`. Requiring the run would make
   those two stop rules mean nothing, since nothing but an option can follow a
   path or a section name. *)

(* FILE INCLUSION SEMANTICS -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P6-001]. `{{ … }}` is a
   PROCESSOR-LEVEL directive, NOT part of the core parser. Its normative
   semantics are below and mirrored, with worked examples, in
   docs/includes.md. A conformant CORE never recognizes the directive
   (unreachable from `block`/`inline`) and leaves `{{ … }}` LITERAL: its
   source is parsed as ordinary inline content, as if no include feature
   existed. A bare-path directive
   with no active inline markers emits verbatim (corpus "Include directive
   with no resolver renders literal"). A directive that is invalid on its
   face, an `@lines` value that is not a valid I1 range, a `@code:lang` value
   that is not a `language_info` token, or a bare-path directive with a bare
   `@code` option, is emitted as its source text with
   no inline parse of its tokens (carve#3803, corpus 715 and 735); emphasis
   or a link around it may still pair across it (carve#3804), and a token
   inside it opens nothing (carve#4296). An opener before it that closes on a
   token inside it breaks the directive, whose text is then read as content:
   `` `a *{{ `@lines:1 }}* `` is a code span and the rest (corpus 855). The rules a conforming PROCESSOR that
   DOES expand includes MUST follow:

   I1 SYNTAX. `{{ path }}`, `{{ path #section }}`, `{{ path @key:value }}`,
      combinable `{{ path #section @key:value }}` (`include_directive`). `path` is bare
      (stops at space / `#` / `@` / `}`) or double-quoted for spaces, and
      is resolved RELATIVE TO THE INCLUDING FILE. Directive parameters have
      three kinds: SELECTION, TRANSFORM and MODE. SELECTION chooses content:
      `#section` (semantic -- a heading's section or one block, I1a) and
      `@lines:N-M` (physical -- a 1-based inclusive line range), and
      `@region:name` (physical -- a named source region, I1b). For `@lines`,
      N and M MUST be decimal integers without leading zeros, N MUST be at
      least 1, and N MUST be no greater than M (a reversed range is invalid).
      Zero and a leading zero are invalid. Any other value is an ERROR
      (`include-lines-invalid`, Warning + literal, per I7); the file stays
      a resolved dependency (I11). A line range
      that STARTS past the file's last line is an ERROR
      (`include-lines-out-of-range`, Warning + literal, per I7); the file
      stays a resolved dependency (I11). A range that starts on or before
      the last line and ends past it selects through the last line. A final
      line ending does not begin a further line, so an empty file has no
      lines, and neither has a file holding only its leading U+FEFF, which
      is not content (I1c). The
      SELECTION mechanisms, these three and `@rows:N-M` (I1d), are MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE: a directive MUST use at
      most one; a directive carrying two is an ERROR (Warning + literal,
      per I7). TRANSFORM options reshape already-selected content and are
      orthogonal to selection: `@cols` (I1d) projects delimited table columns;
      `@shift:N` shifts every included heading's
      level by the SIGNED integer N (I8). `@shift` MAY accompany `#section`,
      a line-range, a region, or neither. `@shift` takes EITHER a signed integer OR
      the literal value `auto` (context-relative, computed by the processor
      per I8), never both. A MODE option chooses what the selected content
      becomes: `@code` and `@code:lang` make it one code block (I1c). Without
      it the content is read as Carve. Every other `@key` is RESERVED.
      An option key appears AT MOST ONCE: the same key given
      twice (`@lines:1-2 @lines:5-6`, `@code:js @code:ts`, `@shift:1
      @shift:2`, or a bare `@code` beside `@code:lang`, which share the key
      `code`) is an ERROR (`include-duplicate-option`, Warning + literal,
      per I7). The last value never silently wins. Like a malformed option,
      it is never passed to the resolver.

   I1a FRAGMENT SELECTION (`#name`). `name` is an `explicit_identifier`
      (`include_section`; see its production), so every explicit id is
      nameable. It is matched the way a `</#id>`
      cross-reference matches (PART 9R R4), against the child's OWN parse, before
      the child's nested includes expand. Two steps, the first that
      matches wins:
      (1) HEADING. A heading whose id (explicit `{#id}` or auto slug) is
          `name` selects its SECTION: that heading and the sibling blocks
          after it in the same block sequence, up to the next heading of
          the same or higher level or the end of that sequence. A heading
          nested in a container is bounded by its container.
      (2) BLOCK. Otherwise a BLOCK carrying the EXPLICIT id `name`
          selects exactly that block, with its own attributes. A block is
          a `blockNode` (PART 12) standing in a block sequence of the
          child's body, at any depth, other than a definition (footnote,
          reference, abbreviation, citation), a comment or frontmatter.
      Within a step, the FIRST match in document order wins (a container
      precedes its descendants). An id carried only by something that is
      not a block in the body -- an inline element, a list item, a table
      row or cell, a definition term, a footnote definition or a block
      inside one -- selects NOTHING: the directive is an ERROR (Warning +
      literal, per I7), as for an id the child does not contain. Every
      other rule treats a block selection as it treats a section: I2 in
      inline position (a selected paragraph splices its inlines and its
      own attributes do not carry into the sentence; any other block is
      block-in-inline), I4 provenance, I5, I6, I8 (T is measured over the
      selected block; no headings in it makes `auto` a no-op), I11 (a
      missing id leaves the dependency RESOLVED).

   I1b NAMED SOURCE REGION (`@region:name`). `name` is an `identifier` and is
      matched exactly. A marker is a whole physical line: optional ASCII
      spaces or tabs, one of the comment forms below, and nothing after it
      but spaces or tabs. The keywords `#region` and `#endregion` are
      case-sensitive: `#Region` is not a marker. Spaces or tabs MAY separate
      a comment opener from the keyword and the name from a comment closer.
      A leading U+FEFF of the file is not content (CARVE-P0-001, I1c), so
      marker recognition skips it in both modes: a file that opens with
      U+FEFF followed by a marker has that marker on line 1.
      WHICH FORMS ARE MARKERS FOLLOWS THE INCLUDE MODE, so a marker never
      renders in the file that holds it:
      (a) An include that reads Carve (no `@code`) recognizes ONLY the `%%`
          line comment: `%% #region name` and `%% #endregion [name]`. A bare
          `#region` line, a `// #region` line, a braced `{% #region name %}`
          comment and every other form are ordinary text in that mode, so
          an include naming such a region finds no region.
      (b) A code include (`@code`, I1c) recognizes the source-comment forms
          `#region name`, `// #region name`, `-- #region name`,
          `/* #region name */`, `<!-- #region name -->` and
          `%% #region name`, and the same forms with `#endregion [name]`. A
          block comment's closer (`*/`, `-->`) MUST end the same line.
      In both modes a `%` run of three or more (`%%% #region name`) is a
      block comment fence, never a marker. A keyword run on by an ASCII
      letter, digit or `_` (`#regional`) is not a marker; run on by any
      other character (`#region-x`) it is a malformed marker. The
      opening marker MUST name a region; a closing marker MAY omit the name.
      Distinct names MAY nest. A named closer MUST match the innermost open
      name. The same name MUST NOT open twice in one file, even sequentially.
      An unmatched, mismatched, unclosed, duplicate or malformed marker makes
      region selection an ERROR (`include-region-invalid`, Warning + literal).
      A missing selected name has the same result. The selected source is the
      physical lines strictly between its matching markers. Markers are found
      by physical line before delimited data is decoded, so a marker line
      inside a quoted field still opens or closes a region: `h` / `"a` /
      `#region r` / `b"` / `#endregion r` with `@region:r` selects the one
      line `b"` (carve#4553). Those two marker
      lines are excluded; nested marker lines remain source content, which a
      table include then reads as no record (I1d). An empty
      region selects an empty source. Positions in selected content retain
      their original source-file coordinates, as with `@lines`. A resolved
      file stays a resolved dependency even if its region is invalid.

   I1c CODE INCLUDE (`@code`, `@code:lang`). A MODE option (I1): the selected
      content becomes ONE code block instead of being read as Carve.
      PAYLOAD. The block's content is the selected source -- the whole file,
      an `@lines` range, an `@region` region, or records selected by `@rows`
      on a delimited table (I1d) -- unparsed as Carve: no Carve
      construct is recognized in it, so a `{{ ... }}` in it is never
      expanded (I9) and a fence line in it is content. Lines are split on
      `newline` as PART 0 defines it, a leading U+FEFF of the file is not
      content (CARVE-P0-001), and every payload line ends with one line
      feed, as an authored fence's content does. An empty selection is an
      empty code block. A selection of N lines is N payload lines, so a
      selected blank last line keeps its line feed: `@lines:1-3` of a file
      holding `x` and two blank lines gives the same three payload lines as
      the whole file.
      SPAN. The block's `pos` (PART 12 §4) is measured in the included file
      and covers the selected lines. It starts at column 1 of the first
      selected line, after the leading U+FEFF when that line is line 1. A
      blank first line still starts the span: it is payload, so
      CARVE-P12-021 does not move the start. The span ends immediately after
      the last codepoint of the last selected line, whose line terminator is
      not included (CARVE-P12-014), so a blank last line ends the span at
      its column 1. Offsets count every codepoint of the file, a CRLF as
      two. An empty selection has a zero-width span where the selection
      begins: the start of the file (after a leading U+FEFF), or column 1 of
      an empty region's closing marker line. A table rendered from the block
      (`{table}`) has the same span.
      LANGUAGE. `@code:lang` gives the block the language `lang`, which MUST
      be a `language_info` token; any other value is a malformed option
      (Warning + literal, I7). A bare `@code` gives the block NO language: it
      is not inferred from the file name or its extension.
      ATTRIBUTES. A block attribute line directly above a BLOCK code include
      applies to the code block exactly as it applies to an authored fence:
      classes, an id, `title` and every other key, and an extension that
      reads a code block (callouts, diff presentation) reads this one.
      SELECTION. `@code` composes with `@lines`, with `@region` (I1b (b)),
      with `@rows` on a delimited table (I1d), or with neither.
      `#section` selects Carve structure and is NOT available:
      `#section` with `@code` is an ERROR (`include-selection-conflict`,
      Warning + literal). `@shift` has no effect, since the payload holds no
      headings (I8).
      POSITION. A code block is block structure, so a code include in INLINE
      position is an ERROR (`include-block-in-inline`, I2).
      CAPTION. A block code include carries the ordinary `caption_slot` after
      its directive line: no blank line or one blank line may precede `^ `.
      The caption attaches to the block the include becomes, the table when
      `{table}` applies to well-formed data, otherwise the code block. When
      malformed delimited data leaves a code block, that block keeps the
      caption. The caption's `pos` is in the including file; the table's or
      code block's `pos` remains in the included file.
      Every other rule is unchanged: resolution and containment (I3, I10),
      cycles, depth and the byte budget (I6), errors (I7) and dependency
      reporting (I11).

   I1d DELIMITED TABLE INCLUDE SELECTION. `@rows:N-M` is a SELECTION on a
      block `@code:csv` or `@code:tsv` include with a `{table}` attribute.
      It counts parsed records from 1, including header records, after
      the leading U+FEFF is removed and the whole file is decoded. A quoted
      field containing newlines is one record. A whole physical line that is a
      well-formed region marker (I1b (b)) is NOT a record, under every selection
      or none: it is no row and no cell, and it does not count toward `@rows`
      or `header-rows`. A malformed marker is an ordinary record unless
      `@region` is selected. With no `@region`, a marker-shaped line inside a
      quoted field is the field's text. N and M follow the `@lines`
      decimal rules in I1: no zero or leading zero, and N no greater than M.
      Invalid values give `include-rows-invalid`; a start past the last
      record gives `include-rows-out-of-range`. The end may pass the last
      record and is clipped. `@rows` is exclusive with `#section`, `@lines`
      and `@region`; a combination gives `include-selection-conflict`.
      The file remains an I11 dependency on every Warning + literal path.

      `@cols:"Name,Other"` is a TRANSFORM on the same table include.
      The name form is double-quoted. It selects columns by exact header
      name and writes them in the given
      order, so it may reorder columns. A backslash quotes a comma or a
      double quote inside a name, following `quoted_value`; an unescaped
      comma separates names. A name absent from the first header record,
      an empty header name, or an ambiguous duplicate header name gives
      `include-cols-invalid`, Warning + literal under I7. Naming one column twice gives
      `include-cols-duplicate`, also Warning + literal. With
      `{table header-rows=0}` only, unquoted `@cols:N-M` selects the inclusive
      1-based numeric column range using the same value rules as `@rows`.
      A malformed or out-of-bounds numeric range gives `include-cols-invalid`.
      `@cols` can accompany any valid selection or no selection. Either
      option outside this table include context gives its `include-rows-invalid`
      or `include-cols-invalid` Warning + literal.

      The first `header-rows` records of the whole file (default one), region
      marker lines excluded, form the header, projected to the chosen columns. A selection chooses only
      body records. A header record inside its range is not repeated.
      `header-rows=0` disables the header and makes every selected record a
      body record. Selected cells keep their positions in the included file;
      projection never rebases them to the directive or a filtered table.
      `@lines` remains valid and counts physical file lines;
      on this table include a linter also emits
      `include-lines-table-rows-hint` (Hint) suggesting `@rows`.
      The directive's caption slot attaches to the resulting table (I1c).
      If delimited quoting is malformed, the include falls back to a code
      block holding the selected source, the full source when nothing selects,
      with `delimited-table-malformed` as usual. Malformed quoting is
      decided first, so `@rows` selects nothing there: the fallback holds the
      full source and reports no `include-rows-out-of-range`. Markdown
      export writes the selected cells in order as a plain GFM pipe table;
      a caption becomes a following paragraph.

   I2 BLOCK vs INLINE. A directive ALONE on its own line is a BLOCK
      include: the resolved content merges as BLOCKS at the directive
      site. A directive within inline content is an INLINE include: the
      resolved content MUST parse (as an I4 fragment) to INLINE-ONLY
      content -- a single paragraph, or nothing -- and its inlines are
      spliced into the surrounding inline sequence. Resolved content
      with any other block structure in inline position is an ERROR
      (Warning + literal, per I7). Mirrors the PART 9 §10 block/inline model.
      A line holding only a `{{ ... }}` directive with `@code` or
      `@code:lang`, at a block start, is a block include by its source
      spelling. It ends at its own line and does not open a paragraph, so a
      following `^ ` line is not paragraph text. Resolution does not change
      this boundary. An unresolved or refused directive, or one with no
      resolver, stays literal as its own paragraph, in I7's sense: its
      source text with no inline parse of its tokens, so `{{ _a_ @code:js }}`
      keeps its underscores. The following caption line is an ordinary
      paragraph. A Carve-content include without `@code`
      keeps the block/inline behavior above.

   I3 RESOLUTION MODEL. The CORE performs NO file I/O. The HOST supplies a
      resolver with the contract: given (path, section?, options?,
      includingFileContext) it returns resolved Carve source text OR an
      error. If NO resolver is configured the directive is left LITERAL
      (CARVE-P6-001).
      An included file whose bytes are not well-formed UTF-8 is non-text
      content and is refused, not replaced: the replacement rule of
      CARVE-P0-023 covers the document handed to the processor, not an
      included file.
      OPT-IN: no resolver is configured by default, and a host MUST leave
      it off for untrusted input unless it has satisfied PART 9 §25 (File
      inclusion).

   I4 MECHANISM (normative on OUTCOME, permissive on mechanism). The
      observable result MUST be AS IF the resolved content appeared inline
      at the directive site, SUBJECT TO FRAGMENT CONTAINMENT: included
      content is parsed as a SELF-CONTAINED FRAGMENT. A construct still
      open at the end of the included content CLOSES at child EOF (an
      unclosed fence or div terminates there, exactly as at the end of a
      standalone document); included content NEVER captures or
      reinterprets parent content that follows the directive. An
      implementation MAY splice source pre-parse OR parse-and-merge
      events / AST -- but ONLY where the result equals the fragment-
      containment outcome. The two diverge exactly when the child ends
      inside an unterminated construct (a naive textual splice would let
      an unclosed child fence swallow the rest of the parent); that
      result is NON-conformant. This matters doubly for `@lines:N-M`,
      whose physical slice can cut a fence or div in half: the torn
      construct is bounded to the fragment, never the parent. Source
      positions SHOULD be remapped so source-mapped hosts can attribute
      included spans to the child file.

   I5 CROSS-FILE COLLISIONS. An identifier that expansion brings together
      is handled according to whether it is DOCUMENT-VISIBLE or FILE-LOCAL.

      DOCUMENT-VISIBLE IDENTIFIERS -- DETERMINISTIC RENAME-ON-COLLISION.
      Two namespaces are document-visible: they are exposed by the
      assembled document and can be targeted across file boundaries.
      (a) ELEMENT IDS. Every EXPLICIT id `{#id}` on ANY element -- a
          heading, any other block (paragraph, list, table, figure, code
          block, div, admonition, ...), a list item, a table row or cell,
          an inline span, link, image or other inline element -- is ONE
          namespace, because HTML has one `id` namespace per document: a
          heading `{#tip}` and a paragraph `{#tip}` collide exactly as two
          headings do.
          Names are compared EXACTLY, case included, as HTML compares
          ids: `{#Tip}` and `{#tip}` do not collide.
      (b) FOOTNOTE LABELS (footnotes are collected and numbered globally
          in the assembled document). This is a SEPARATE namespace: a
          footnote `[^tip]` never collides with an element `{#tip}`.
      ORDER is the fully expanded document read top to bottom: parent
      before child, and an earlier include before a later include. An
      occurrence is RENAMED when an EARLIER occurrence of the same name in
      the same namespace came from a DIFFERENT file inclusion (another
      file, or another inclusion of the same file); the first occurrence
      keeps its name. Duplicates that one file already held on its own are
      not created by expansion and are not this rule's business.
      EVERY RENAMED OCCURRENCE TAKES ITS OWN SUFFIX: the least `-N`
      (integer N >= 2) such that the result is used by NO explicit name of
      that namespace anywhere in the fully assembled document -- the
      parent, earlier and later includes, the child itself -- and by no
      earlier rename. A child holding a colliding `{#d}` twice therefore
      yields `d-2` and `d-3`, never two `d-2`s, and a `{#d-2}` written
      after the include pushes the rename to `d-3`. A Warning MUST be
      emitted per renamed occurrence.
      REFERENCES FOLLOW THE RENAME. A reference written in the SAME file
      inclusion as the renamed target, that resolves to that target when
      the file is read on its own, resolves to the new name instead
      (where that file held several renamed copies, to the FIRST, the
      one the file reaches when read on its own):
        - a `</#id>` cross-reference (PART 9R R4);
        - a link or image destination that is exactly `#` followed by the
          id, whether written inline (`[text](#tip)`) or reached through
          that file's own reference definition (`[a]: #tip`, file-local,
          below);
        - a footnote reference `[^label]`, for a renamed footnote label.
      A reference in ANY OTHER file inclusion keeps the name as written,
      and therefore reaches the first occurrence: naming an id the parent
      also defines is how a child links to the parent's target. A
      fragment link to an id its own file does not define is never
      rewritten.

      FILE-LOCAL IDENTIFIERS -- SCOPED, NOT RENAMED. A REFERENCE-DEFINITION
      label is file-local metadata: it is resolved WITHIN the document that
      defines it, BEFORE that document is merged into its parent. Two files
      MAY therefore use the same label with different destinations, and each
      file's references resolve to its own definition -- a parent `[a][]`
      resolves to the parent's `[a]: /PARENT` and a child `[a][]` to the
      child's `[a]: /CHILD`. This is NOT a collision: it MUST NOT emit a
      Warning and MUST NOT rename anything.

      SCOPE: the include-time rename pass covers EXPLICIT element ids (on
      any element, headings included) and footnote labels ONLY.
      Auto-generated (slug) heading-id collisions are NOT part of it and
      emit no Warning; they continue to dedup via the existing heading-id
      tracker (PART 9 §13, which suffixes duplicate slugs once the files are
      merged). ORDERING: the include-time explicit-id / footnote rename runs
      BEFORE the PART 9 §13 slug dedup, so ids are deterministic (the explicit-id
      namespace is settled first, then PART 9 §13 dedups auto-slug ids against the
      final set).

   I6 LIMITS. The processor MUST detect inclusion CYCLES (a file that
      transitively includes itself): the offending directive is left
      literal, not expanded, and a Warning is emitted. Cycle detection is
      the PRIMARY guard. As a SECONDARY DoS bound, implementations MUST
      enforce a FINITE include-depth limit; the RECOMMENDED default is at
      least 16 (a small, human-scale nesting bound, far under the structural
      MAX_NESTING = 200 of PART 9 §25) and the host MAY configure it. No exact hard
      constant is pinned. A directive at greater depth is left literal with
      a Warning. Total expanded output is charged against the same per-
      render byte budget as other amplifying features -- `max(1 MB,
      8 x input length)` (PART 9 §25); once exceeded, further expansion degrades
      to the literal directive with a Warning. The byte budget bounds the
      expanded OUTPUT; it does NOT bound the WORK done to produce it,
      because a target is resolved before its size is known and a document
      may carry one directive per dozen bytes. Implementations MUST
      therefore enforce a FINITE bound on RESOLVER INVOCATIONS per render;
      the RECOMMENDED default is at least 1000, and the host MAY configure
      it. A directive past the bound is left literal with a Warning and
      MUST NOT be passed to the resolver. The byte budget and the
      resolver-call bound are whole-render TOTALS that only ever grow, so
      once either is exhausted, later directives MUST be refused BEFORE
      invoking their resolver -- not resolved and then refused -- and the
      two bounds therefore limit reads as well as emitted output.

   I7 ERRORS. Each include error emits a Warning and leaves the directive
      literal, as CARVE-P6-001 defines it: a directive invalid on its face
      keeps its source text with no inline parse of its tokens. Inclusion
      NEVER silently drops a directive. See PART 9 §25
      (File inclusion) for the security model: parser stays pure, resolver is host-supplied and MUST enforce
      canonical path containment after symlink resolution and MUST NOT
      fetch remote URLs without an explicit allowlist. Included content
      receives the same sanitization as its parent. The processor-neutral
      vectors in `tests/include-security-conformance/` pin these security
      requirements separately from the broader include-language suite.

   I8 HEADING-LEVEL SHIFT (`@shift:N` | `@shift:auto`). A transform option
      orthogonal to selection (I1).
      LITERAL FORM. N is a SIGNED integer; every heading in the included
      content has its level increased by N (N=2: an h1 becomes an h3; a
      NEGATIVE N raises levels, N=-1: an h2 becomes an h1). An omitted
      `@shift` is `@shift:0` (no shift, the default; current behavior
      unchanged). CLAMP: the resulting level is clamped to the valid range
      [1, 6]; a shift that would push a heading below 1 or above 6 clamps to
      the bound and emits a Warning -- the heading is KEPT, never dropped.
      IDS/SLUGS UNCHANGED: `@shift` changes only the level, never a
      heading's id / slug (ids are name-based), so `</#id>` cross-references
      into shifted headings still resolve. AUTO-NUMBERING: if section
      auto-numbering (HeadingNumbers, docs/extensions.md) is enabled,
      shifted headings renumber at their NEW level as a consequence.

      AUTO FORM (`@shift:auto`). CONTEXT-RELATIVE: the processor COMPUTES
      the shift instead of taking it from the author.
      CONTEXT LEVEL C: the level of the nearest PRECEDING heading, in
      document order, that is in the directive's OWN block container or an
      ENCLOSING (ancestor) container. A heading inside a SIBLING container
      that has already CLOSED does NOT set the context. If no such heading
      exists, C = 0.
      TOP LEVEL T: the MINIMUM heading level present in the resolved
      content, AFTER any `#section` or line-range selection has been
      applied. The MINIMUM (not the first heading's level) is used so the
      included document's internal relative structure is preserved.
      COMPUTED SHIFT: N = (C + 1) - T. N is then applied EXACTLY as a
      literal shift under the rules above -- clamp to [1, 6] with a Warning
      on clamp, heading KEPT never dropped, ids / slugs unchanged so
      cross-references still resolve, and auto-numbering renumbering at the
      new level.
      NO HEADINGS: if the resolved content contains NO headings, `auto` is a
      NO-OP (N = 0) and emits NO Warning.
      NESTED INCLUDES: `auto` resolves against the document AS ASSEMBLED at
      that point, so a shift already applied by an ANCESTOR include is in
      effect when a nested `auto` is computed. Expansion proceeds
      OUTSIDE-IN, which makes the result DETERMINISTIC.
      INLINE INCLUDES: inline content cannot contain headings, so `auto` on
      an INLINE include (I2) is a no-op.
      EXAMPLE: an including document with `## Chapters` before the directive
      gives C = 2; a child starting at `# Chapter One` gives T = 1; so
      N = (2 + 1) - 1 = 2 and the child's h1 renders as an h3.

   I9 VERBATIM PROTECTION. An include directive is recognized ONLY where
      inline and block constructs are recognized. Inside a code span, a
      fenced or raw code block, or any other verbatim context, `{{ ... }}`
      is ordinary LITERAL text and is NEVER resolved. This is a consequence
      of Carve's general invariant that CODE IS VERBATIM -- no construct
      transforms inside a code span or code block -- of which the include
      directive is no exception. A literal `{{ ... }}` can therefore always
      be written and displayed by placing it in code.

   I9a RECOGNITION SHAPE -- RUN, NOT NODE. Grammar-level recognition does
      NOT mean "one parsed text node matches the directive grammar". A
      directive's own syntax overlaps constructs the core already parses:
      `#section` is TAG syntax, `@key:value` is MENTION syntax, and a
      double-quoted path is rewritten by smart quotes, so by the time the
      include pass runs `{{ x #intro @shift:1 }}` is SEVERAL adjacent nodes.
      A processor MUST recognize a directive over a CONTIGUOUS RUN of inline
      nodes carrying only LITERAL TEXT content (text, tag, mention, and any
      other node whose source form is recoverable verbatim): reassemble the
      run's source and match the directive against it. Matching single nodes
      would drop every directive carrying `#section` or an option, and every
      directive appearing mid-sentence. The run STOPS at
      any node that is not literal-text-shaped, so a directive interrupted
      by emphasis, a link, or a code span is NOT recognized and stays
      literal (same rule as a bare-path directive with active inline
      markers).

   I10 CONTAINMENT ROOT + CANONICAL CONTAINMENT CHECK. Every resolved path
      is checked against a single CONTAINMENT ROOT.
      DEFAULT ROOT (RECOMMENDED): for FILE-BASED entry points (a CLI
      invocation on a document path, or a convert-from-file API) the root
      SHOULD default to the DIRECTORY OF THE TOP-LEVEL DOCUMENT -- a
      document path is already known, so requiring the host to name a root
      explicitly is not necessary. The root MUST NOT default to the process
      working directory (arbitrary with respect to the document, and
      possibly `/` or a home directory).
      NO PATH CONTEXT, NO DEFAULT: for STRING-INPUT APIs there is no
      document path and therefore no inferable root; a host MUST supply the
      root explicitly, otherwise inclusion stays DISABLED and directives
      remain LITERAL (I3). This preserves the opt-in posture for embedders.
      A CONFIGURED ROOT MUST BE ABSOLUTE: any other root spec is REFUSED,
      leaving inclusion DISABLED and directives LITERAL as with no root.
      The test is absoluteness, not emptiness: a whitespace-only spec is
      refused as relative, and an absolute path to a directory named with
      spaces is a valid root.
      ONE ROOT PER EXPANSION: relative include paths resolve relative to the
      INCLUDING file (I1), but containment is checked against the single
      TOP-LEVEL root. The root MUST NOT re-base per included file; a
      re-based root shrinks at every nesting level, so a nested document
      could never reference a sibling directory of the project.
      CANONICAL, NOT LEXICAL (MUST): a processor MUST canonicalize the
      candidate path (resolving symbolic links) and then verify the
      canonical result is contained within the canonical root. A `..`
      segment is PERMITTED exactly when the canonical result stays inside
      the root. A lexical `..` rejection is wrong on both sides: TOO STRICT
      (it rejects legitimate sibling-directory layouts, e.g. a document in
      `chapters/` including `../shared/glossary.crv` whose target is inside
      the project root) and TOO WEAK (symbolic links and absolute paths
      escape a root with no `..` present at all). Absolute paths remain
      DENIED unless they canonicalize inside the root. Given the layout
      `book/{main.crv, chapters/ch1.crv, shared/glossary.crv}` with root
      `book/`: from `chapters/ch1.crv`, `../shared/glossary.crv` is ALLOWED
      and `../../../etc/passwd` is DENIED. See PART 9 §25 (File inclusion)
      and docs/security.md.
      A containment refusal MUST NOT reveal whether the target exists.
      For a missing path, canonicalize the longest existing prefix and
      normalize the remaining path before checking containment. Present
      and absent targets outside the root MUST both be refused as
      containment denials, without reading or checking the existence of
      the target itself first.

   I11 DEPENDENCY REPORTING. A processor that expands includes MUST report,
      alongside the expanded document and any Warnings, the SET OF INCLUDE
      TARGETS it touched during the expansion.
      SCOPE: the set covers the ENTIRE recursive expansion, including
      nested children, DE-DUPLICATED, in a DETERMINISTIC order.
      IDENTITY: each target is identified by the resolver's CANONICAL ID
      where one is supplied -- the same identity the cycle guard uses in
      I6 -- and otherwise by the RESOLVED PATH. Two spellings that name
      the same document MUST return the same id (`a/b.crv`, `a/./b.crv`).
      An unresolved target's id is resolved against the including file
      like a resolved one, so it names where the file would appear; a
      path that escapes the containment root (I10) keeps the directive's
      spelling. So does a target that exists but the resolver refuses.
      So does a request carrying a URI scheme, which names no place.
      ATTEMPTED TARGETS ARE INCLUDED: the set MUST contain targets that
      were ATTEMPTED but NOT resolved -- missing or unreadable, binary,
      containment-denied (I10), cycle-broken (I6), and depth- or
      size-exceeded (I6).
      DISTINGUISHABILITY: each entry MUST be distinguishable as RESOLVED
      versus ATTEMPTED-BUT-UNRESOLVED.
      Hosts use this contract for invalidation and diagnostics; see
      docs/includes.md (Reported dependencies, and Editor and IDE integration).

   I12 THE WRITER PRESERVES A DIRECTIVE VERBATIM. A Carve writer MUST NOT
      escape the braces of a well-formed directive; it writes the directive
      as spelled, including one whose shape is well-formed but whose option
      is unknown or whose `#section` names no id. Inside a
      verbatim context `{{ … }}` is not a directive (I9).

   I15 A PROCESSOR TARGETING CARVE SOURCE DOES NOT EXPAND. When the output
      target is Carve source (`fmt`, a `--carve` render, any pipeline whose
      product is Carve), the expansion pass MUST NOT run, whether or not a
      resolver is configured. Other targets expand as usual.

   I16 LINT EXPANDS INCLUDES. A linter MUST expand include directives when
      the caller gives it an include root or a resolver, and MUST NOT read
      any include target when the caller gives neither; without one it
      reports nothing about include targets. Expansion for lint follows the
      same rules as expansion for rendering: root containment (I10), the
      byte budget, the resolver-call bound, the depth limit and the cycle
      guard (I6).
      WHAT RUNS. The top-level document is linted as it is without
      includes. Every Carve-mode include the expansion accepts is linted
      with every lint rule and with the options the top-level document is
      linted with, over the source the expansion parsed for it: the whole
      file, the `@lines` range or the `@region` region. For a `#section`
      selection the whole file is linted and only the findings on the
      selected lines are kept, from the first line of the selected heading
      or block through the last line of the last selected block. A rule
      that reads the payload of a block also runs on a `@code` include's
      block, once the attribute line above the directive applies to it
      (I1c): `delimited-table-malformed` and `delimited-table-wide-row` run
      on a `{table}`-marked `csv` or `tsv` code include.
      WHERE A FINDING POINTS. A finding in included content names the
      included file by its I11 identity, with a line and a column counted
      in that file, so content selected by `@lines` or `@region` keeps its
      source coordinates. A payload finding points at column 1 of the line
      where the offending record starts: for `delimited-table-malformed` the
      first record whose quoting cannot be read, for
      `delimited-table-wide-row` the first record with more fields than the
      first record. A finding in included content also carries its INCLUDE
      CHAIN: the directives that reached the file, the top-level document's
      directive first, each named by its file, line and column.
      DUPLICATES. A finding in included content is reported once per
      distinct file, line, column and rule. When one position is reached
      more than once, the first reach in document order supplies the chain.
      A file included twice therefore reports each finding once, while two
      selections of different ranges of one file each report the findings
      in their own range. A transform option such as `@shift` does not make
      a range distinct.
      INCLUDE ERRORS. Every Warning that leaves a directive literal (I7) is
      a lint finding under its own rule id (`include-unresolved`,
      `include-non-text`, `include-region-invalid`,
      `include-selection-conflict`, `include-cycle` and the rest), at the
      directive in the file that wrote it, with the chain that reached that
      file. Duplicates collapse as above. The order in which findings are
      reported is not part of this rule. *)
